+
+/// How the runtime makes a texture over a buffer whose rows (or an array's slices) lie with bytes
+/// between them that are not its texels, and which renders or which a shader writes (its usage
+/// holds `RenderTarget`, `ShaderWrite`, `ShaderAtomic` or block writes): an IOSurface wider than
+/// its rows' alignment, a display's framebuffer among them. Metal never writes those bytes; a
+/// render pass's load and store operations may write back any byte of an attachment's
+/// subresource ("read and write back any memory locations within the image subresource bound for
+/// that attachment"), which lavapipe does, so the exact form is a shadow (the default).
+#[non_exhaustive]
+#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
+#[cfg_attr(feature = "serde", derive(serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize))]
+pub enum PaddedTargetsPolicy {
+    /// The runtime's shadow image of the texels, copied in from the buffer before each use and
+    /// back after each write (the default): no byte between the rows is ever read or written
+    /// through an image.
+    #[default]
+    Shadow,
+    /// An image over the buffer's bytes at Metal's row pitch, holding the bytes between the rows in
+    /// its subresource, as a texture that only reads takes under [`PaddedReadsPolicy::Alias`]:
+    /// rendered and written in place, with no copy in or back. The bytes between the rows stay the
+    /// program's only as far as the driver's moves, loads and stores leave them, which a consumer
+    /// takes this for where it checked its driver does.
+    Alias,
+}
